+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a dependency or weighs how to build a feature. New services must justify their operating
+burden on larder's volunteers. The "one more service" argument came up again in issue 70 after the session-store
+and job-queue debates; this page records the rule those proposals share so the next proposal can start from it.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Add a service we operate only when Postgres cannot do the job and two volunteers can restore it.
+
+In 2023 Redis was rejected for sessions because only one volunteer would have run it. Redis was later used for
+Celery, but in March 2025 it evicted 300 receipt jobs; only one volunteer could restore it. The decisions to
+[keep sessions in the database](adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adr/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) apply this tenet; those records hold
+the alternatives and consequences.
+
+In [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), pantry search took four seconds and missed "Rice, brown" when searching for
+"rice". Elasticsearch was proposed, but Postgres full-text search on the item name was reported to take 40 ms and
+find the item, so the proposer chose Postgres instead. Being able to set up a service over a weekend did not answer
+who would restore it when its proposer was away.
+
+The default is one database and one web app, as the [architecture](architecture.md) describes. A proposal for a new
+service the volunteers would operate must show both what Postgres cannot do and that two volunteers can restore
+the service. This rules out adding a service for a job Postgres can already handle, or leaving recovery dependent
+on one volunteer. External services remain an option: larder uses a card gateway, and a hosted service that no
+volunteer has to operate is acceptable.
